WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:16.634
Privacy and security in Open WebUI begin with recognizing what the system may hold: prompts, outputs, uploaded files, conversation history, user accounts, API keys, endpoint configuration, logs, database backups, and operational metadata.

00:00:16.634 --> 00:00:19.919
Any of these may contain sensitive information.

00:00:19.919 --> 00:00:25.160
A private AI interface is still an application that needs secure operation.

00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:36.412
API keys and endpoint credentials should be protected because they may allow access to model services, billing resources, private endpoints, or internal systems.

00:00:36.412 --> 00:00:43.681
Avoid exposing credentials in screenshots, shared notes, logs, or configuration files with broad access.

00:00:43.681 --> 00:00:49.132
Rotate credentials when needed and remove old keys when endpoints are retired.

00:00:49.132 --> 00:00:54.164
Plugin and tool features should be approached carefully at a high level.

00:00:54.164 --> 00:01:04.089
Anything that lets a model interact with external systems, files, APIs, or automation increases the need for permission boundaries and review.

00:01:04.089 --> 00:01:09.610
Model output should not be treated as authority to perform high-impact actions.

00:01:09.610 --> 00:01:14.991
Keep tool use scoped, monitored, and appropriate to the deployment's purpose.

00:01:14.991 --> 00:01:19.954
Operations include updates, monitoring, backups, and incident response.

00:01:19.954 --> 00:01:26.523
Watch resource usage, disk growth, failed requests, unusual access, and model endpoint errors.

00:01:26.523 --> 00:01:32.184
Keep the application and supporting components updated with a deliberate process.

00:01:32.184 --> 00:01:45.463
If accidental exposure or misconfiguration occurs, operators should know how to disable access, preserve evidence, rotate credentials, restore from backup, and notify the right stakeholders.
